<p>Philippine Senator Ronald “Bato” dela Rosa, a former national police chief wanted by the International Criminal Court, left the Senate building where he had taken refuge to avoid arrest, Senate President Alan Peter Cayetano and other officials said Thursday. Cayetano said the Senate sergeant-at-arms confirmed dela Rosa was no longer inside, while Presidential Communications Undersecretary Claire Castro said officials were seeking confirmation after several sources said he had left the premises. The departure followed gunshots late Wednesday at the Senate, where people ran for cover and lawmakers sheltered in offices; officials reported no immediate casualties. The ICC unsealed an arrest warrant Monday for dela Rosa on allegations of crimes against humanity linked to killings during Rodrigo Duterte’s anti-drug campaign, allegations he has denied. NPR reported that Philippine police were investigating the incident, including suspicions that the chaos was staged to help dela Rosa escape.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Qs_qd_eqdJPuPDt_IBCvoWPcgHHcZEoxjg4d2E744WZ3edn4QKZbjgsv_8Gu3haYIUz7M0RsACta7_1GZUvv9az0YVxQW9psqxHnS_XtCqj1oyiPcgcWZTDkKYo77oYdXUuYzBnLgCPVB0noR6xFhF8FgaDVgbwOOZuS6uWvWWJIYSsUG-oPlTWy340mU8XUNNvS_ihey9vzgn3suRKLQa0SbiuWwGpGtYf5cDKPvMPWEGhxXGamhYl2RL7KnGGNeN_8tDh4gZRc' alt='Security guards and police officers keep people back after gunshots were heard at the Philippine Senate in Manila.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Warrant details: The ICC warrant, originally issued in November, accuses dela Rosa of murder as a crime against humanity involving “no less than 32 persons” between July 2016 and the end of April 2018.</li><li>Security dispute: Senate Secretary Mark Llandro Mendoza said law enforcement officers believed to be from the National Bureau of Investigation tried to enter and fired as they retreated, but NBI Director Melvin Matibag told GMA News no agents had been deployed.</li><li>Official response: President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. held an emergency meeting with security chiefs after the Senate gunfire, while police said one person had been detained and evidence including spent casings and a rifle magazine was recovered.</li><li>Whereabouts: Thai PBS reported Cayetano received a message from dela Rosa’s wife apologizing for what happened but not disclosing where he had gone, as officials worked to track him.</li></ul><blockquote>Do not allow another Filipino to be brought to The Hague - Sen. Ronald dela Rosa</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Alan Peter Cayetano: The Senate president said dela Rosa left voluntarily and argued that the senator could be arrested only if a Philippine judge issued an order. (<a href='https://www.eldiario.es/internacional/disparos-caos-e-incognitas-aliado-duterte-buscado-corte-penal-atrinchera-senado-filipino-huye_1_13221123.html'>El Diario</a>)</li><li>Claire Castro: The presidential communications undersecretary said there had been no operations launched so far to arrest dela Rosa. (<a href='https://interaksyon.philstar.com/politics-issues/2026/05/14/313440/philippines-seeking-confirmation-dela-rosa-wanted-by-icc-has-fled-official-says/'>Philstar</a>)</li><li>Drug-war victims’ families: Relatives of people killed in the drug war gathered outside the Senate to demand dela Rosa’s arrest and that he be brought to justice. (<a href='https://www.thaipbs.or.th/news/content/505905'>Thai PBS</a>)</li><li>Dela Rosa&#x27;s lawyer Jimmy Bondoc: Bondoc said after speaking with dela Rosa late at night that he believed the senator remained inside the building and had told him he did not intend to leave. (<a href='https://www.cna.com.tw/news/aopl/202605140183.aspx'>CNA</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/southeast-asia/article/3353591/philippine-senator-wanted-icc-flees-senate'>Philippine senator wanted by ICC flees from Senate</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/05/14/g-s1-122021/philippines-senator-escape-w